If there is a programme for young specialists, perhaps it matters how many people without professional connections received their first paid experience. If there is a competition for new cultural voices, perhaps it matters how many first-time applicants reached substantive evaluation and what actually filtered out the others. If people have a legal right to a benefit, perhaps the core metric should not be the amount of published guidance but the share of eligible people who received it and the structure of those who disappeared on the way.

Administrative burden then stops being “the user somehow failed to cope” and becomes a property of the route: **“our process systematically filters out people like this.”** Only then can the more useful question be asked: are these really the people we intended to filter out?

## Humanisation, or perhaps something deeper?

Up to this point we have been talking about **humanisation**: making institutions more understandable, accessible, proportionate, less traumatic and more attentive to real human trajectories. That is already substantial. Near the end, however, another suspicion appears. Humanisation can still sound like maintenance of the existing machine: keep its basic logic intact, make the buttons larger, simplify the language and add an adviser.

What if, in some cases, we need more than humanisation? What if we need what this essay calls **humanation**, a shift in the centre of gravity in which the person stops being merely “the user of a process” and the process becomes an instrument of a human purpose again? A form does not exist so that a form may be completed, a portal so that a portal may have users, a fund to manufacture grant applications, a university to manufacture academic procedures, or a social service to manufacture evidence that information about assistance was made available.

Humanation would not make the human being the centre of the universe or abolish the system’s right to rules. It could mean a more mature reciprocity: a system has the right to set conditions, a person has the right to agency, and rules must continuously remember **which human purpose they are serving**. When a rule no longer protects that purpose and primarily reproduces itself, perhaps the rule should have to pass an examination again.

## The architecture of possibility

We began with an artist, then met the junior candidate with three years of experience, the young researcher whose first grant helps produce the next, the person required to demonstrate administrative competence to receive support at the very moment resources are lowest, and the digital system that can simplify access or merely move the old labyrinth onto an OLED screen. At some point it becomes clear that this is not an essay about grants, HR, welfare offices, art or even bureaucracy. It is about **the architecture of possibility**: who may move from “not yet” to “already”, who pays for the transition, who carries uncertainty, who is allowed to fail, how many times, and whether the absence of a previous victory is treated as evidence of future incapacity.

The MET[Ȧ]CADEMY Manifesto insists that an abandoned path may have been abandoned not because it was false but because an instrument, money, language, time, political will or a sufficiently stubborn successor was missing. It proposes preserving not only the history of what humanity discovered but also the history of what humanity **might have discovered**. In social terms, a difficult analogue follows: **an unrealised person is not the same thing as a person without potential**. Sometimes it is a person whose entry point was constructed in such a way that the future never obtained permission to become an experiment. [MET[Ȧ]CADEMY OF HUMANITY Manifesto v1.2](https://d4ttara.github.io/metacademy-of-humanity/manifesto/).

## The future has no CV

A human-centred, perhaps eventually humanational system would probably permit more **correct mistakes**. Someone will receive a chance and waste it, someone will receive a small grant and produce mediocre work, a company will train a junior who leaves six months later, a researcher will test a beautiful hypothesis and get zero, someone will start a business and close it. This is a real cost of an open entry point, and pretending otherwise would be unethical.

The alternative also has a cost. A system can be designed to make very few errors at entry. There is a simple method: admit primarily those who have already proved they should be admitted. Such a system will be stable, predictable, professional, efficient and nearly error-free. It may also slowly lose the ability to see what is not yet in its database. The future has one very inconvenient administrative property: **the future has no previous experience of being the future**.

Artificial intelligence now intensifies the problem. If statistical systems become increasingly accurate at predicting who will become a successful artist, researcher, employee, founder, student or “reliable citizen”, that accuracy could help direct resources where they are likely to work. The same accuracy could also create the most sophisticated system of doors in history, doors that almost never open for the “wrong” person. Document 015 asked what happens when AI correctly recognises a type but the error lives in the type itself. Document 016 adds another question: **what happens when a prediction describes the past so well that it begins deciding who is allowed to have a future?**

We are used to asking whether a particular person deserves a chance. Perhaps sometimes the camera should turn around: **what kind of system deserves the right to decide who receives a first chance?** If tomorrow we can calculate with near-perfect accuracy which people are most likely to make good use of an opportunity, will we use that precision to support those for whom entry is harder, or will we finally build perfect doors that never open for a statistically inconvenient person?

And if the second option proves cheaper, more efficient and more accurate, **who will notice the people we could have become but did not become because the system learned to predict us too well before the first attempt?**

## Sources and research anchors

This research essay develops principles from the MET[Ȧ]CADEMY OF HUMANITY Manifesto v1.2 concerning the absence of monopoly by any single mode of seeing, traceable provenance, preservation of abandoned paths and the right of systems to revise their own frames. Manifesto: https://d4ttara.github.io/metacademy-of-humanity/manifesto/.

For cultural funding: Julia Peters & Henk Roose, “The Matthew effect in art funding. How reputation affects an artist's chances of receiving government support”, *Poetics* 92, 2022, DOI: https://doi.org/10.1016/j.poetic.2021.101578. For the classical Matthew effect: Robert K. Merton, “The Matthew Effect in Science”, *Science* 159(3810), 1968, DOI: https://doi.org/10.1126/science.159.3810.56. For cumulative advantage in science funding: Thijs Bol, Mathijs de Vaan & Arnout van de Rijt, “The Matthew effect in science funding”, *PNAS* 115(19), 2018, DOI: https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1719557115.

For the first-experience trap and internship quality: ILO, “Internships: Head start or labour trap?”, https://www.ilo.org/resource/article/internships-head-start-or-labour-trap; Niall O’Higgins & Luis Pinedo, “Interns and outcomes: Just how effective are internships as a bridge to stable employment?”, ILO Employment Working Paper No. 241, 2018, https://www.ilo.org/publications/interns-and-outcomes-just-how-effective-are-internships-bridge-stable.

For administrative burden: Pamela Herd & Donald Moynihan, “Administrative Burdens in the Social Safety Net”, *Journal of Economic Perspectives* 39(1), 2025, DOI: https://doi.org/10.1257/jep.20231394. For experimental evidence on information and application assistance: Sarika Gupta, “Perils of the Paperwork: The Impact of Information and Application Assistance on Social Benefit Take-Up in India”, *American Economic Journal: Economic Policy* 18(3), 2026, DOI: https://doi.org/10.1257/pol.20240212. For digital access, linked administrative data and automatic enrolment: OECD, *Modernising Access to Social Protection: Strategies, Technologies and Data Advances in OECD Countries*, 2024, DOI: https://doi.org/10.1787/af31746d-en.

For an example of explicit uncertainty handling in research funding: Health Research Council of New Zealand, “2026 Explorer Grants”, where qualifying applications are randomly ordered for funding until the available budget is exhausted: https://gateway.hrc.govt.nz/funding/ideas-funding/2026-explorer-grants.

